\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,booktabs}
\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}\newtheorem{theorem}[lemma]{Theorem}
\newcommand{\Q}{\mathbb Q}\newcommand{\Z}{\mathbb Z}
\title{A spectral obstruction and three remaining cases}\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
We consider finite simple quartic graphs of girth six in which every
edge lies on exactly one hexagon. The fixed source F012 gives the lower
bound $57$ for their order. We prove here that order $51$ is impossible.
The remaining order $54$ is a separate obligation; this argument alone
does not recover the source's bound.

\begin{lemma}\label{ball}
Every vertex lies on two hexagons. Its distance spheres through radius
three have sizes $1,4,12,34$. The graph joining opposite vertices on
hexagons is simple and has degree two.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each hexagon uses two edges at a vertex, and the four incident edges
are covered exactly once, so there are two hexagons. Girth six gives
four distinct neighbours and twelve distinct second neighbours. The
$36$ nonbacktracking paths of length three end at distance three: a
shorter distance would give a cycle of length at most five.
Two such paths to the same endpoint cannot share their first edge,
since after deleting a common initial segment they would give a cycle
of length at most four. Nor can they meet earlier and separate again.
Their union is therefore a hexagon. There are precisely two pairs of
coincident endpoints, one for each hexagon through the root. Three paths
to one endpoint would put the first edge of one path on two hexagons,
which is forbidden. Hence the sphere has $36-2=34$ vertices, and the
two hexagons have different opposite vertices. Joining these opposite
pairs thus gives a simple graph of degree two.
\end{proof}
Counting vertex--hexagon incidences gives $6c=2n$, so $3\mid n$.
Lemma~\ref{ball} gives $n\ge51$; consequently only $51$ and $54$ can
occur below $57$.

\begin{theorem}\label{main}
There is no such graph on $51$ vertices.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose that one exists, with adjacency matrix $A$. Let $M$ be the
adjacency matrix of the opposite-pair graph in Lemma~\ref{ball}.
Every radius-three ball is the whole graph. Write $D_i$ for its
distance-$i$ matrix. The absence of triangles and quadrangles gives
$D_2=A^2-4I$. For adjacent vertices there are seven walks of length
three: the two backtracking forms each give four, with their common
walk counted twice. For vertices at distance two there are none,
since a nonbacktracking walk would give a cycle of length at most five.
For vertices at distance three, there is one such walk, or two exactly
when they are opposite on a hexagon. Therefore
\[
 D_3=A^3-7A-M,\qquad
 M=A^3+A^2-6A-3I-J.                                    \tag{1}
\]
Put $P(x)=x^3+x^2-6x-3$. Since $A$ is symmetric and regular,
$M$ commutes with $A$. On the rational invariant subspace
$\mathbf1^\perp$, their eigenvalues satisfy $\delta=P(\theta)$.

For $m\ge3$ put $\delta_m=\zeta_m+\zeta_m^{-1}$, where
$\zeta_m$ is a primitive $m$th root of unity. The finite certificates
below prove that $P(x)-\delta_m$ is irreducible over $\Q(\delta_m)$
for $3\le m\le51$, $m\ne10$. They also prove irreducibility for
$\delta=2$ and $\delta=-2$ over $\Q$.
Here is the exact deduction from each certificate. Its prime $p$ and
residue $z$ have $z$ of order $m$ in $\mathbb F_p$, and
\[
 P(x)\ne z+z^{-1}\quad\text{for every }x\in\mathbb F_p. \tag{2}
\]
A root in $\Q(\delta_m)$ would be an algebraic integer, since it
satisfies a monic polynomial with algebraic-integer coefficients.
It therefore belongs to $\Z[\zeta_m]$, by the classical cyclotomic
integer-ring theorem \cite[Theorem~6.4(b)]{Milne}.
The substitution $\zeta_m\mapsto z$ defines a homomorphism to
$\mathbb F_p$: $p\equiv1\pmod m$, and $z$ is a primitive $m$th root.
It would send that root to a solution of (2), a contradiction.
A reducible cubic over a field has a root. This proves the asserted
irreducibility. For $\delta=\pm2$, reduction modulo two gives
$x^3+x^2+1$, which has no root in $\mathbb F_2$.

The nonprincipal characteristic polynomial of $A$ is rational.
For a root $\theta$ with $P(\theta)=\delta_m$, the field
$\Q(\delta_m)$ is contained in $\Q(\theta)$, with relative degree
three. Thus all three roots over each fixed $\delta_m$ are conjugate
over $\Q$ and occur with equal multiplicities. The multiplicity of
$\delta_m$ as an eigenvalue of $M$ on $\mathbf1^\perp$ is consequently
a multiple of three. This applies also to $\delta=2$.

As $M$ is simple and has degree two, it is a union of cycles of
lengths $3$ through $51$. Let $c_l$ be their numbers and
$c=\sum_l c_l$. The eigenvalue $2$ has multiplicity $c$ on the whole
space, hence $c-1$ on $\mathbf1^\perp$. For $m\ge3$, the chosen
primitive eigenvalue $\delta_m$ occurs twice in a cycle precisely
when $m$ divides its length. It follows that
\[
 c\equiv1\pmod3,\qquad
 \sum_{m\mid l}c_l\equiv0\pmod3
 \quad(3\le m\le51,\ m\ne10).                         \tag{3}
\]
Descending from $51$ to $11$ proves $c_l\equiv0$ for every $l>10$.
The equations for $m=9,8,7,6,4,3$ then give the same conclusion for
those six lengths. The equation for $m=5$ gives $c_5+c_{10}\equiv0$.
Adding the cycle counts now gives $c\equiv0$, contradicting (3).
No assertion about the exceptional polynomial for $m=10$ is needed.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Exact finite certificates.}
The table gives $(m,p,z)$. For $m=1,2$ the third entry is omitted and
$\delta=2,-2$, respectively. Each prime is checked by trial division;
order is checked by $z^m=1$ and $z^{m/r}\ne1$ for every prime divisor
$r$ of $m$. Every residue in (2) is checked, rather than a sampled
subset. The $50$ rows require $3880$ residue evaluations; the largest
prime is $311$. An independent modular combination of (3) is also
retained as a check on the final contradiction. These finite checks
supplement the field and multiplicity arguments above.
