\paragraph{The remaining order.}
For order $54$, the radius-three ball contains $51$ vertices and there
are three exterior vertices. There is only one rooted shape for the
edges on the length-three paths. Start with a rooted tree having four
children at its root and three children at every later nonleaf vertex,
through depth three. Pair the four root branches according to the two
hexagons. In each branch exactly one root-to-leaf path participates in
its paired hexagon. Rooted subtree permutations send that path to the
first child at each step. Identify the selected leaf in each pair of
branches. This constructs a fixed graph $R$ on $51$ vertices with
$52$ edges, and every graph under consideration contains it with its
root fixed. Extra edges within the ball are not presumed absent.

There are $34$ boundary vertices: $32$ have degree one in $R$ and two
have degree two. All other vertices are already saturated. The induced
graph on the three exterior vertices is triangle-free, hence is empty,
a single edge, or a two-edge path, up to their independent relabelling.
Every still undecided edge joins two boundary vertices or joins a
boundary vertex to an exterior vertex. These are exactly the permitted
pairs in the three literal inputs produced by \texttt{root\_inventory.py}.
If the chosen exterior graph has $e$ edges, the degree sums also give
\[
 e(B,Y)=12-2e,\qquad e(B)=44+e.                         \tag{4}
\]
These identities are recorded as checks; no additional unproved filter
is used to restrict the permitted-pair relation.

The inventory checker verifies all seven labelled triangle-free graphs
on three vertices against these three representatives. It also constructs
literal injective copies of $R$ at each of the $84$ vertices of the
acquired positive example. The normalization proof above supplies general
coverage; the example does not supply a completeness premise.
The separately reviewed forced-hexagon recursion can be applied to each
of these inputs. If all three executions finish with no completion,
Theorem~\ref{main} and Lemma~\ref{ball} will yield the source bound $57$.
No such execution or exclusion is asserted in this note.

The cyclotomic integer-ring theorem is retained classical input.
The graph identities, their application to these cycle multiplicities,
and the listed finite certificates are supplied here. No priority or
current conjecture-status claim is made.
